I missed the original post but.. 
How's this for a horror story.
I've got a number of perl scripts to handle incoming mail.  I wrote them
a long time ago to use mh on an ultrix machine when 'slocal' was 
totally broken.  We recently upgraded one of our DS5000/240 machines with
10k+ users to an AXP 3000/400.  Everything seemed to be going ok
as far as the perl scripts go until the other day I realized I wasn't
getting notified of new mail.  I checked and I had about 15 perl processes
stuck in the background, and the mailq showed at least that number of mail
messages waiting to be delivered.  I discovered that unlike ultrix,
osf does not clean up it's ttys when people log out.  It leaves them owned
by a given person until someone else takes the tty.  Ultrix and just about
every other Os I've seen chowns them to root when someone leaves.

Sooo..  I realized the problem was that my perl script was trying to write
to all ttys owned by me.. big mistake.. it froze on every tty owned by me
but not in use by me.  I started killing perl processes..  Killed
the sh -c perl.... as sendmail calls it.. and then the associated perl
process.. BOOM.. the machine dies.. Not sure if it's coincidence I run
into work and try it again.. BOOM.. the machine goes down again.. Call up
the admin.. "Errr.. I think I may be crashing the machine  ... Want to kill
some processes?"  WE managed to kill it 5 times.  Got 4 good crash logs
and hopefully field support can do something with it.  Anyway, something
about unused ttys, sendmail and perl.... Just say no.

Other than that only about a hundred things were broken after the upgrade.
I read user's help mail so I get to see them all.. ;)
Enhanced security is a bit sticky. 
ntalk and talk ports are swapped..
lack of non-dec terminals in /etc/termcap
osf/1 uses ^H, ultrix uses ^?
osf/1 has a serious problem detecting connection speed with telnet from ultrix
  stty everything shows 0 baud if you telnet from ultrix to osf/1, even on
  axposf.pa.dec.com)   We put  stty dec -tabs erase ^? 38400   in the system
  login to fix a variety of problems..

Ok.. maybe not hundreds..  It's been fun though..

	ian


