Or because a lot of us with Alphas are too busy to post ;-\

We have 3 alphas - a 3000/500x that was here before I was, and 2
3000/400's that arrived about 2 months late.

The first thing I noticed as I began to install them was "Open VMS" on
the bill of lading. (They were ordered with OSF/1).  Fire the first
one up, sure enough -- Open VMS was pre-loaded.  Just for the hell of
it, I tried the second one, hoping for OSF.  This one was completely
DOA nothing appeared on the monitor, and nothing would appear on an
alternate console.

Well, a DEC service man showed up the same day with an OSF/1 cd.  We
got the one running OpenVMS re-installed with OSF/1 that day, and the
next week the DOA machine had gotten a new motherboard  and was
running OK.  However, both machines needed firmware upgrades to be
fully compatable with OSF/1.  The one that booted originally needed a
new motherboard to accept the firmware update, and the DOA machine
needed a framebuffer.  All this was finished about 2 months after we
got the machines.  We still don't have OSF/1 boot cd's, or docs..

OSF/1 1.3 itself (at least as implemented by DEC) is a dog.  32M of
memory is totally inadequate for a single user workstation.  The boot
prom (of all things) eats up about 2M, and the OS another 6, leaving
about 24 for user programs.  Rebuilding the kernel when you have
neither docs, nor a boot cd is a real adventure..

The location of programs, strange startup procedure, and the horrible
forrest of symbolic links takes a little getting used to if you are
coming from a BSDish environment like SunOS or Ultrix, but its much
better than Solaris.

We have had a few crashes, mainly dealing with NFS bugs, or from
running out of swap space, but the machines have usually stayed up for
weeks at a time.

As I'm sure you have noticed by reading this group, porting software
to this 64bit architecture is no fun either.  Some things work right
out of the box, but others take hours or days.  Third party software
is still sparse (as compared to the sparc world). 

Don't get me wrong, these are wonderful, fast machines, and I wouldn't
hesitate to recommend them for serious number crunching jobs.
However, make sure you get enough memory.  And don't totally rely on
these machines for your general purpose computing needs.  And don't
rely on DEC to fix (or ship) anything unless you remind them often..

I'm seriously considering IBM or HP the next time we buy something.

Drew

