> However, one thing puzzling me and my colleagues here is the following.
> Alpha normally appears much faster than Ultrix on ordinary duties.
> However,  whenever the C compiler is being invoked to compile a
> package,  the whole system slows down significantly.  The response
> is much slower,  even though there is only one user.  It seems that
> Alpha dedicated too much time and priority to the compilation job.
> This happens on both lasy swap and dedicated swap modes.

I see this sort of thing whenever I do disk-intensive thingies. For example,
if you have a couple of really big files and execute a shell script to 
infinitely copy them to /dev/null, after a couple of minutes the machine is
pretty much gone. It's as if the disk cache takes over all of memory or 
something.

Another esoteric thing that I've seen involves interrupt latency. When a 
process that has allocated a lot of memory exits, the system sees a long
interrupt latency while the process is cleaned up. Under the real-time
kernel with a process that allocates and touches 80MB of memory, the
interrupt latency can be around 150 milliseconds. DEC found the problem
and it will be fixed in 2.0, but the system will probably still not be
able to schedule new jobs during that 150 milliseconds; it will only be
able to take interrupts.

My other gripe is that you can no longer mmap() a device. Even though it
is possible on the TURBOchannel, I have yet to find a combination of
flags, etc. for the mmap(2) call that causes the device driver's mmap()
entry to be invoked. Of course, even if the driver's mmap() entry _were_
invoked, there is no documentation about what it's supposed to do...
