In article <755380895@juliet.cs.duke.edu>, kenkel@duke.cs.duke.edu (Stephen Kenkel) writes:
> 
>   I'm having an odd problem with a DEC 3000  Alpha running
> OSF-1.  The NLS (Native Language support_) routines work fine
> for "joe blow user", but not for "root".  For example, I can create
> a catalog file with "gencat"
>    % gencat file.cat file.msg
> 
> As myself I can examine the file with "dspcat"
>    % dspcat file.cat 1
>      message ...
>      message ...
>      message .
>    
>    BUT, as root, I can't look at the file
>    %su
>    %dspcat file.cat 1
>      Unable to open specifed catalog (file.cat)

The NLS routines do not reference the NLSPATH environment variable when
executing as a privileged program (i.e. setuid to root) as this
represents a wide variety of interesting security holes. Depending on
what version of the code DEC has, it may also disallow the NLSPATH 
variable if logged in or su-ed to root (as appears to be the case).
A case can be made for allowing a "real" root user (logged in) to use
NLSPATH, but it is difficult to protect 'su' from "Trojan catalogs".

{Un}fortunately, dspcat is implemented using catopen(), so it gives you
the messages you would have seen if you had run the particular command
and generated its messages.


