>
>However, when I try to use "disklabel" to rewrite the partition table, here's
>what happens:
>
>   CARIBOU> disklabel -e -r rz9
>    [ make my changes to partition table ]
>   write new label? [y]: y
>   disklabel: ioctl DIOCSDINFO: Open partition would move or shrink
>   Use alternate partition
>   re-edit the label? [y]:
>
>What's going on?  What's with the reference to an "open partition"?  Opened
>by what?
>

This took me a while to figure out ... I had this problem when
changing the a partition on the boot disk. I had to mop boot (across
the network) to get it fixed ...

It seems the mere existance of the device file in /dev can cause
the device to be "open". Since it seems your disks are external,
i'd boot into single user mode, delete /dev/rz9[abdefgh], do
the disklabel thing, and re MAKEDEV the devices.

>And, given that disklabel doesn't want to let me write the new label to my
>not-in-use disk, how *can* I repartition it?  Will I have to boot from the
>distribution CD?  As usual, many TIA ...

If the above suggestion doesn't work, you will prolly need to boot
from the distribution CD:

* chose system maintenance mode
* cd /dev
* MAKEDEV rz9
* rm rz9[abdefgh]
* disklabel
* halt and reboot





