Ed McQuillen writes: 
> I'm looking for any horror stories about DEC's 3000/* series.

Do I have stories!  I received a DEC 3000 Model 400 back in August.  It was
nothing but a major headache for almost four months. 

Things didn't get off to a good start when the Alpha we got wasn't the Alpha
ordered.  For my work, we need 24-bit color.  What we got was the 8-bit 
OPEN3D card, and worse it wasn't even installed even though they charged
us $500 to install it on the invoice.  

After installig the card , I quickly learned that not all the pre-installed 
software was installed (in fact, less than half of what the included sheet
said was on the hard drive was actually there) including the OPEN3D software.
This warranted a quick call to DEC.  In the meantime, the machine was
running in console mode.  Then, the monitor starting going haywire.  It looked
like something out of Outer Limits.  Another call to DEC.  The next day,
the service man comes to take a look.  His suggestion is that it's too hot
where I work (it's about a consistent 80 degrees in my old office).  First, 
I tried to use a fan trying to circulate the air around the machine to no
avail.  The problems continue until a couple days later when the IO board
kicks the bucket.   A weekend later (this happened on a Friday), the 
service man is back and replaces the IO board.  Things are working again!
But, he notices these strange dots that litter the screen after the 
display card test.  He calls back later to say that he thinks its the 
memory on the card not working right.  So, yet another visit corrects
this problem.  

Now, the overheating problems continues.  As an experiment, I move the 
Alpha to another, cooler office.  Everything seems to be fine.  So, we
kick someone out of their office, and I move in.  The funny thing is that
even my old office temperature is supposed to be withing the specs of the
CPU.  ("Well, I guess those are going to have to re-write them," is the 
reply from DEC).  

To speed things up, in the next three months there are a total of 75 crashes
either due to more overheating problems or kernel panics.  One day our
sales rep comes by to find out how things are.  I tell him.  Well, he
offers us a replacement machine with the specifications of the machine
we wanted in the first place.  It arrived a week ago.  Everything that 
was supposed to be there is there, including the 24-bit color card (though, 
when I first turned on the machine nothing came out on the monitor; it 
turns out the card wasn't pressed down enough for contact).  

One week later, things are working well. No crashes or overheating.  I 
hold onto my belief our first machine was a lemon with the display card
the major culprit.  I'm happy now, but it was a long time in coming.

You wanted horror stories,
--Keith A. Scollick



